# Line endings, declared rather than inherited from the machine.
#
# # WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# The `Conformance vectors regenerate byte-identically` gate compares the committed
# `conformance/vectors.json` with a freshly generated one, byte for byte. On a GitHub Windows runner
# it failed with `regeneration CHANGED the file` while passing on Linux, macOS and on a developer's
# Windows machine.
#
# The cause was not the generator. `conformance/generate.mjs` writes `JSON.stringify(...) + '\n'`,
# which is LF. The cause was git: with no `.gitattributes`, Windows checks out text files with CRLF
# by default (`core.autocrlf=true`), so the committed file arrived at the runner as CRLF and the
# regenerated one was LF. The bytes differed, and the gate was right to say so.
#
# So the file being compared was not the file that was committed -- and the gate that noticed is the
# only reason this was ever seen. A byte-identity check is worth exactly as much as the checkout it
# reads from.
#
# This is the second failure of the same shape in two releases. The first was
# `scripts/verify-all.mjs` building a shell command with `process.execPath` -- `C:\Program
# Files\nodejs\node.exe` -- where `cmd` split the path at the space. Both were environment
# differences between a developer's machine and a runner, both looked arbitrary from the outside, and
# both were invisible locally for the same reason: **the author's environment happened to be the one
# the code assumed.**
#
# # THE RULE
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` makes every text file check out with LF on every
# platform. That is the right default for this repository because its artefacts are compared as
# bytes -- `vectors.json` here, and the SHA256SUMS of the release assets -- and a checkout that
# silently rewrites bytes makes those comparisons meaningless.
#
# Binary files are marked and left alone. Rewriting a `.png` or a compiled artefact is how a
# repository acquires subtly corrupt assets that nothing notices until they are used.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- default
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- explicit text types
# Named rather than left to detection, because these are the files whose BYTES matter.
*.rs       text eol=lf
*.toml     text eol=lf
*.lock     text eol=lf
*.md       text eol=lf
*.json     text eol=lf
*.mjs      text eol=lf
*.js       text eol=lf
*.ts       text eol=lf
*.py       text eol=lf
*.yml      text eol=lf
*.yaml     text eol=lf
*.sh       text eol=lf
*.sol      text eol=lf
*.txt      text eol=lf
*.html     text eol=lf
*.css      text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
Makefile   text eol=lf
VERSION    text eol=lf

# `vectors.json` is the reason this file exists. Called out by name so that a future change to the
# catch-all above cannot quietly exempt it.
conformance/vectors.json text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- binaries: never touched
*.png      binary
*.jpg      binary
*.jpeg     binary
*.gif      binary
*.ico      binary
*.pdf      binary
*.zip      binary
*.gz       binary
*.tgz      binary
*.exe      binary
*.dll      binary
*.so       binary
*.dylib    binary
*.a         binary
*.rlib     binary
*.wasm     binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- generated, and self-declaring
# `Cargo.lock` IS committed here (the release build uses `--locked`), so it is text above rather than
# ignored. Left in this section as a note: the usual advice to omit it is wrong for a binary crate
# whose releases must be reproducible.
#
# `contracts/lib/` is a Foundry dependency fetched by CI, never committed -- see `.gitignore`.
# Marked binary so that a stray vendored file cannot be line-ending-rewritten if it ever appears.
contracts/lib/** binary
